<%#
  The three historical trend pages -- donations, purchases, distributions -- were identical apart
  from a title and a series, and each was a bare Highcharts config with no page header, no card and
  no <h1>. Their controllers had been given `layout "essentials_app"` and marked migrated; the
  views were never touched.

    render "historical_trends/trend", title: title, series: series

  The chart carries no title of its own: the page header is the h1, and the chart repeating it gave
  the page two headings that could drift apart.
%>
<% content_for :title, "#{title} - #{current_organization.name}" %>

<div class="px-4 py-6 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
  <%# These three are listed in the reports catalogue and are in no menu, so the hub was the only
      way in and there was no way out. %>
  <%= render "shared/essentials/page_header",
        title: title,
        subtitle: "Cached, so it may be up to 24 hours behind.",
        back: {path: reports_path, label: "Back to reports"} %>

  <%# The window goes in the filter bar, where every other report's date filter goes -- not in the
      header's actions, which is a right-aligned row sized for buttons. %>
  <%= render "shared/essentials/filter_bar", url: request.path, auto_submit: true do %>
    <div class="min-w-0"><%= render partial: "shared/month_range_picker" %></div>

    <%# One control for what the page is about -- categories and items in one searchable list.
        It replaces the single-select that was here and the checkbox that was on every table row. %>
    <div class="min-w-0"><%= render "shared/compare_picker", categories: @item_categories, items: @series %></div>
  <% end %>

  <div class="space-y-6">
    <%
      # The window comes from the controller now, and with it the two things a reader has to be
      # told about the *edges* of a monthly chart: whether the last column is still running, and
      # whether anything is dated past it. See `HistoricalTrendService`.
      months = @months.map { |m| m.strftime("%b %Y") }
      short_months = @months.map { |m| m.strftime("%b %y") }
      in_progress = @last_month_in_progress
      monthly_totals = @monthly_totals
      grand_total = monthly_totals.sum
      # Stack by category only when there is more than one to stack. An organization that does not
      # use categories gets the single-series chart that was approved as option A, unchanged --
      # which is also what a reader gets the moment they narrow to one category.
      # **No stacked breakdown.** One band per category is a series count taken from the data, and
      # the data does not bound it -- see "A chart's series count is a constant" in design.md. What
      # the reader chose is what the chart draws, at most four of them.
      picked = @comparison_series
      table_items = @table_items
      # One total per month, summed across every item. The per-item figures are in the table below.
      # The y axis said "Line items" before. It is the unit these figures are counted in, and it
      # belongs in the tooltip and the subtitle rather than sideways down the left edge.
      unit_label = "line items"
      chart_subtitle = if picked.any?
        "#{picked.map { |p| p[:name] }.to_sentence}, #{months.first} to #{months.last}."
      else
        "Everything, #{months.first} to #{months.last}."
      end
      chart_subtitle += " #{months.last} is still running." if in_progress && picked.empty?
      if @previous_totals
        # Said in words, because a percentage on its own reads as a quantity and a coloured arrow
        # is a signal only a sighted reader gets. See design.md, never colour alone.
        change = trend_change_phrase(grand_total, @previous_totals.sum)
        chart_subtitle += " Total #{number_with_delimiter(grand_total)}, #{change}." if change
      end
    %>

    <%#
      **One series, not forty-seven.**

      This drew one column series per item, every one of them created `visible: false` -- so the
      page opened with an 850px box, a legend of 47 names and no data at all until you pressed
      "Select all". Pressing it was worse: 47 series across 12 months in a 1034px plot is about
      **1.8px per bar**, keyed by a 191px legend over a ten-colour palette, so five different items
      shared every swatch and the legend could not identify a bar even in principle.

      The chart's job on this page is the month-to-month shape -- how much, and when. "Which item"
      is answered completely by the table directly beneath it, which holds all 47 by month, and a
      top-8 cut would have set a partial answer beside a complete one. See "A chart answers one
      question" in design.md.

      No legend, one colour, and the figure printed on each column so it can be read exactly rather
      than estimated off an axis.
    %>
    <%#
      Comparing sits on the chart, not in the filter bar. **It is not a filter** -- it narrows
      nothing, it adds a reference series -- and the bar folds everything behind a disclosure past
      two controls, which is a measured rule: two plus the gap is ~530px, and three do not fit at
      the narrowest width the table stays a table. Putting a view option there would have hidden the
      range, the control this page was just given.

      A link rather than a checkbox, so it works with no JavaScript and reads as what it does.
    %>
    <% compare_toggle = capture do %>
      <%= essentials_link_button(
            compare_previous? ? "Stop comparing" : "Compare with the previous period",
            request.path + "?" + request.query_parameters.deep_merge("filters" => {"compare" => (compare_previous? ? nil : "1")}).to_query,
            variant: :secondary, size: :sm, icon: compare_previous? ? "bi-x-lg" : "bi-arrow-left-right") %>
    <% end %>

    <%= render "shared/essentials/card", title: "Total per month", subtitle: chart_subtitle,
          actions: compare_toggle do %>
      <% if grand_total.zero? %>
        <%= render "shared/essentials/empty_state", kind: :cold_start,
              title: "Nothing to chart yet",
              body: "Nothing was recorded between #{months.first} and #{months.last}. Widen the range, or choose a different category." %>
      <% else %>
        <%
          previous = @previous_totals
          # The month still running is drawn in a lighter fill with a border. It is never the only
          # signal: the axis label, the table header and the subtitle all say "so far" too, and a
          # fill is a colour by another name.
          mark_last = lambda do |values|
            values.each_with_index.map { |value, i|
              (in_progress && i == values.size - 1) ? {y: value, color: "#A5B4FC", borderColor: "#4F46E5", borderWidth: 1} : value
            }
          end

          chart_series =
            if picked.any?
              # At most four, and told apart by **dash pattern and word**, not by colour: of 28
              # pairs from an eight-colour candidate set, none clears 3:1 under normal vision and
              # three kinds of colour blindness. Each colour is >= 3:1 against the white plot,
              # which is the separate thing 1.4.11 asks for.
              picked.each_with_index.map { |item, i|
                {type: "line", name: item[:name], data: item[:data],
                 color: MonthRangeHelper::PLOT_COLOURS[i], dashStyle: MonthRangeHelper::PLOT_DASHES[i],
                 lineWidth: 2.5, marker: {enabled: false}}
              }
            else
              [{type: "column", name: "All items", color: "#4F46E5", data: mark_last.call(monthly_totals)}]
            end
          if previous
            chart_series += [{type: "line", name: "Previous period", data: previous}]
          end

          chart_height = (previous || picked.any?) ? 360 : 320
          config = {
            chart: {type: "column", height: chart_height, spacingBottom: 8, style: {fontFamily: "Figtree, system-ui, sans-serif"}},
            title: {text: nil},
            credits: {enabled: false},
            xAxis: {categories: months.each_with_index.map { |m, i| (in_progress && i == months.size - 1) ? "#{m}\nso far" : m },
                    crosshair: true, lineColor: "#E2E8F0", tickColor: "#E2E8F0",
                    labels: {style: {color: "#475569", fontSize: "12px"}}},
            yAxis: {min: 0, title: {text: nil}, gridLineColor: "#E2E8F0",
                    labels: {style: {color: "#475569", fontSize: "12px"}},
                    # The total on top of a stack. A label on each segment is unreadable, and the
                    # total is the figure the single-series chart prints on its columns -- so the
                    # same number stays on screen whichever shape the chart is in.
                    stackLabels: {enabled: false, style: {fontSize: "11px", fontWeight: "500", color: "#334155", textOutline: "none"}}},
            tooltip: {shared: true, valueSuffix: " #{unit_label}"},
            plotOptions: {
              column: {borderWidth: 0, borderRadius: 3, pointPadding: 0.08, groupPadding: 0.12,

                       # A data label on every segment of a stack is unreadable; the total on top
                       # of the stack is the number a reader wants, and Highcharts draws that from
                       # the stackLabels below.
                       dataLabels: {enabled: picked.empty?, style: {fontSize: "11px", fontWeight: "500", color: "#334155", textOutline: "none"}}}.compact,
              # The previous period is a line over the columns rather than a second set of columns:
              # two column series a month is the crowding this chart was rebuilt to escape. Dashed
              # and grey, so it reads as background against the period being looked at.
              line: {color: "#94A3B8", dashStyle: "ShortDash", lineWidth: 2,
                     marker: {enabled: false}, enableMouseTracking: true}
            },
            legend: {enabled: !!(picked.any? || previous), align: "left", verticalAlign: "bottom",
                     itemStyle: {fontWeight: "500", color: "#334155"}},
            series: chart_series
          }.to_json
        %>
        <%= render partial: "shared/highcharts",
              locals: {config: config, height: chart_height,
                       label: "#{title}: total per month, #{months.first} to #{months.last}#{" against the previous period" if previous}"} %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <%#
      The same figures as a table. design.md: a chart is never the only representation of the data,
      because a chart is not readable by a screen reader and not printable in colour. These three
      were the only chart pages in the app and none of them had one, so the rule had nowhere it was
      actually being kept.

      The trend column is the chart the summary above cannot be: 47 items, each with its own shape,
      no legend and no colour spent. See `essentials_sparkline`.
    %>
    <%#
      What the window does *not* include, said out loud. A distribution can be dated ahead of
      itself, and a trend is what happened rather than what is booked -- so those records are out
      of the series by design, and dropping them without a word would make the chart quietly wrong
      for anyone who knows they exist. Zero for donations and purchases, which are recorded after
      the fact, so the callout does not appear on those pages at all.
    %>
    <% if @scheduled_beyond_today.to_i.positive? %>
      <%= render "shared/essentials/callout", tone: :info do %>
        <%= number_with_delimiter(@scheduled_beyond_today) %> line items are scheduled for after
        today and are not counted here. This is what has happened, not what is booked &mdash;
        <%= link_to "the distributions list", distributions_path, class: "font-medium underline" %>
        shows those.
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <%#
      No form and no checkboxes. Choosing is done in the Compare control above -- ticking a row here
      cost a full page load and 1,702px of lost scroll, so four choices were four reloads and four
      scroll-downs. The table is a table again.
    %>
    <%= render "shared/essentials/card", padded: false, title: "By item and month",
          subtitle: selected_comparisons.empty? ? nil : "Narrowed to what you are comparing." do %>
      <% if table_items.blank? %>
        <%= render "shared/essentials/empty_state", kind: :cold_start,
              title: "Nothing to show yet",
              body: "Once there is activity, every item appears here with its monthly figures." %>
      <% else %>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Table, scrollable">
          <table class="data-table dense">
            <caption><%= title %>, by item and month</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Item</th>
                <th scope="col" class="trend">Trend</th>
                <% short_months.each_with_index do |month, i| %>
                  <%# The month still running is named as such in the header too. A hatched column
                      and a footnote are not enough on their own: this is the cell a reader
                      compares, so the word belongs on the cell. %>
                  <th scope="col" class="quantity">
                    <%= month %><% if in_progress && i == short_months.size - 1 %><span class="block text-[11px] font-normal text-slate-500">so far</span><% end %>
                  </th>
                <% end %>
                <th scope="col" class="quantity">Total</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <% table_items.each do |item| %>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" class="font-medium text-slate-900"><%= item[:name] %></th>
                  <td class="trend"><%= essentials_sparkline(item[:data], months: months) %></td>
                  <% item[:data].each do |value| %>
                    <td class="quantity"><%= number_with_delimiter(value) %></td>
                  <% end %>
                  <td class="quantity font-medium text-slate-900"><%= number_with_delimiter(item[:data].sum) %></td>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><%= selected_comparisons.empty? ? "All items" : "These #{table_items.size}" %></th>
                <td class="trend"><%= essentials_sparkline(monthly_totals, months: months) %></td>
                <% monthly_totals.each do |value| %>
                  <td class="quantity"><%= number_with_delimiter(value) %></td>
                <% end %>
                <td class="quantity"><%= number_with_delimiter(grand_total) %></td>
              </tr>
              <%#
                The comparison belongs in the table too. A chart is not the only representation of
                its data, and a dashed grey line is exactly the kind of thing a reader who cannot
                see it needs written down -- so the previous period is a row of figures, with the
                change stated in words rather than left to be worked out from two totals.
              %>
              <% if @previous_totals %>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" class="font-normal text-slate-500">
                    Previous period
                    <span class="block text-xs"><%= @previous_window.first.strftime("%b %Y") %> – <%= @previous_window.last.strftime("%b %Y") %></span>
                  </th>
                  <td class="trend"><%= essentials_sparkline(@previous_totals, months: months) %></td>
                  <% @previous_totals.each do |value| %>
                    <td class="quantity font-normal text-slate-500"><%= number_with_delimiter(value) %></td>
                  <% end %>
                  <td class="quantity font-normal text-slate-500"><%= number_with_delimiter(@previous_totals.sum) %></td>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
